        <div id="before-you-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Check Before You Click Buy</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>The model, not the photo.</strong> Pad listings are reused and renamed. We found one listing that had
            been a Strol 2E now describing a different UREVO pad. Read the bullets for <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt size</a>, incline and weight limit.</li>
            <li><strong>The seller.</strong> On Amazon, check that the seller is the brand or Amazon, especially when stock is
            short and a third party becomes the default seller.</li>
            <li><strong>Coupons.</strong> Many Amazon pad deals need a coupon box ticked on the listing; the price in your
            basket is the one that counts.</li>
            <li><strong>The sibling model.</strong> The Strol 2E and 2E Pro, the W50 and W50 Lite, and the Z1 and Z1R are
            different machines at different prices.</li>
            <li><strong>Space and floor.</strong> Measure the spot under your desk and plan for a mat; our <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a> covers sizing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Once it arrives, register it (UREVO’s extra warranty depends on binding the pad in its app), keep the box until
            the return window closes, and lubricate on schedule. If anything goes wrong, our <a href="/walkingpad-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">WalkingPad troubleshooting</a> and <a href="/urevo-walking-pad-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">UREVO troubleshooting</a> pages list the makers’ own
            fixes before you start a return.
          </p>
